{
  "requirement": "Before that commit, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to revert the drafts the user rejected or deferred and to list them in their report.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has three testable obligations. The `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to (1) revert the drafts the user rejected or deferred, (2) list those drafts in the report, and (3) do both before the commit.\n\nThe test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reverts_rejected_and_deferred_drafts_before_committing]` covers all three:\n- It finds the commit step by locating the step of `habeas-requirements` that contains \"all groups together\". The helpers `step_of` and `step` return the step text, and `workflow_steps` collapses its continuation lines. That is why the sentence in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, which wraps across lines in the source, can be matched as one phrase.\n- It then calls `assert_text_in_order` with two phrases. The first is \"Revert drafts the user rejected or deferred (`git restore -p`) and list them in your report\". The second is \"then commit it\". This one phrase asserts both the revert instruction for rejected or deferred drafts and the instruction to list them in the report.\n- `assert_text_in_order` panics if a phrase is missing or comes before the previous one. The order check therefore enforces that the revert comes before the commit.\n\nViolating behaviors the test would catch:\n- Dropping the revert instruction.\n- Dropping the \"list them in your report\" part.\n- Moving the revert after the commit.\n- Removing the revert from the commit step.\n\nThe test has a {test} reference to the requirement, so there is no marking gap. The requirement has no other modes or boundary cases that need tests.",
  "references": "045e59937558c1b7978d67b970fe4e49f3e1b32fc75671f4a1ffda5028700b2a",
